# Line endings and diff behaviour.
#
# Normalise everything on the way in and check everything out with LF. Shell
# scripts and mise file-tasks are executed directly; a CRLF line ending makes
# the kernel look for an interpreter named "bash\r" and the failure message
# names neither the file nor the carriage return.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Executable text. Called out explicitly rather than relying on `text=auto`
# detection, because a script that a heuristic misclassifies as binary is
# checked out byte-for-byte, CRLF and all.
*.sh       text eol=lf
*.bash     text eol=lf

# Generated artifacts.
#
# `linguist-generated=true` keeps a file out of the language statistics and
# collapses it by default in a pull-request diff — still expandable, just not
# in the way. Neither of these is ever hand-edited: CHANGELOG.md is written by
# @semantic-release/changelog from the commit range, and a lockfile is written
# by its package manager.
#
# CHANGELOG.md also takes `-diff`, which suppresses the textual diff entirely.
# That is safe here precisely because it is derived: it restates commits that
# are themselves reviewable, so nothing is lost by not diffing it.
CHANGELOG.md       linguist-generated=true -diff

# Lockfiles get `linguist-generated=true` and DELIBERATELY NOT `-diff`.
#
# `-diff` marks a file binary for diff purposes, and git then reports only
# "Binary files a/bun.lock and b/bun.lock differ" — locally AND in review. For
# a lockfile that is exactly the wrong trade: the lockfile diff is the single
# place where a changed resolution, a substituted registry or an unexpected
# transitive dependency becomes visible to a human. Collapsing it is helpful;
# hiding it is a supply-chain review gap.
#
# This is not hypothetical. Adding one test-only dependency to this repository
# silently split the tree, pinning a second copy of a package under
# semantic-release to satisfy a peer range. Under `-diff` that change was
# invisible; without it, one line of diff said so.
#
# bun.lock in particular is a TEXT file, so there is no technical reason to
# treat it as binary at all.
bun.lock           linguist-generated=true
package-lock.json  linguist-generated=true
pnpm-lock.yaml     linguist-generated=true
yarn.lock          linguist-generated=true

# The one genuinely binary lockfile: bun's pre-1.2 format. `binary` is correct
# here because the bytes really are not text.
bun.lockb          linguist-generated=true binary
